→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n    84→  },\n    85→  {\n    86→    \"title\": \"DeepSeek V4—almost on the frontier, a fraction of the price\",\n    87→    \"description\": \"Chinese AI lab DeepSeek released V4 series preview models (DeepSeek-V4-Pro and DeepSeek-V4-Flash). Simon Willison analyzes their performance and pricing positioning as near-frontier models at significantly lower costs than Western competitors.\",\n    88→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    89→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    90→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/deepseek-v4/\",\n    91→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-24\",\n    92→    \"topics\": [\n    93→      \"LLM\",\n    94→      \"AI pricing\",\n    95→      \"Chinese AI\",\n    96→      \"model releases\",\n    97→      \"DeepSeek\"\n    98→    ],\n    99→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   100→  },\n   101→  {\n   102→    \"title\": \"Claude Code quality postmortem: agentic systems are fragile\",\n   103→    \"description\": \"Anthropic published a postmortem on Claude Code quality issues. Three separate harness bugs (not model issues) caused problems, including a bug that cleared thinking context every turn instead of once. Simon highlights the complexity of building agentic systems.\",\n   104→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   105→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   106→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/claude-code-quality/\",\n   107→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-24\",\n   108→    \"topics\": [\n   109→      \"Claude\",\n   110→      \"coding agents\",\n   111→      \"AI safety\",\n   112→      \"system design\",\n   113→      \"postmortem\"\n   114→    ],\n   115→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   116→  },\n   117→  {\n   118→    \"title\": \"Honker: Postgres NOTIFY/LISTEN semantics for SQLite\",\n   119→    \"description\": \"A Rust SQLite extension implementing transactional outbox pattern for SQLite, enabling queue and stream semantics with NOTIFY/LISTEN-style functionality. Uses WAL mode polling for near-real-time updates.\",\n   120→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   121→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   122→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/honker/\",\n   123→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-24\",\n   124→    \"topics\": [\n   125→      \"SQLite\",\n   126→      \"databases\",\n   127→      \"queues\",\n   128→      \"transactional outbox\",\n   129→      \"Rust\"\n   130→    ],\n   131→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   132→  },\n   133→  {\n   134→    \"title\": \"Bluesky's For You feed: 72K users on a gaming PC\",\n   135→    \"description\": \"spacecowboy's Bluesky For You feed serves 72,000 users from a single Go process on a gaming PC (16 cores, 96GB RAM, 4TB NVMe) with SQLite storing 90 days of data (419GB). Total cost: $30/month. Could theoretically scale to all 1M Bluesky users.\",\n   136→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (citing spacecowboy)\",\n   137→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   138→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/24/bluesky-for-you/\",\n   139→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-24\",\n   140→    \"topics\": [\n   141→      \"SQLite\",\n   142→      \"scaling\",\n   143→      \"Go\",\n   144→      \"Bluesky\",\n   145→      \"architecture\",\n   146→      \"cost efficiency\"\n   147→    ],\n   148→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   149→  },\n   150→  {\n   151→    \"title\": \"Tool Attention Is All You Need: Dynamic Tool Gating for Agentic Workflows\",\n   152→    \"description\": \"Paper proposing 'Tool Attention' mechanism with dynamic tool gating and lazy schema loading to eliminate the MCP/tools tax in scalable agentic workflows, addressing performance overhead in tool-using AI agents.\",\n   153→    \"author\": \"Anuj Sadani, Deepak Kumar\",\n   154→    \"source\": \"Paper\",\n   155→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21816\",\n   156→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-24\",\n   157→    \"topics\": [\n   158→      \"agentic workflows\",\n   159→      \"tool use\",\n   160→      \"MCP\",\n   161→      \"LLM optimization\",\n   162→      \"arXiv\"\n   163→    ],\n   164→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   165→  },\n   166→  {\n   167→    \"title\": \"From Research Question to Scientific Workflow: Agentic AI for Science Automation\",\n   168→    \"description\": \"Paper exploring leveraging agentic AI to automate scientific workflows from research question to execution, potentially transforming how scientists conduct research by automating complex multi-step processes.\",\n   169→    \"author\": \"Bartosz Balis, Michal Orzechowski, Piotr Kica, Michal Dygas, Michal Kuszewski\",\n   170→    \"source\": \"Paper\",\n   171→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21910\",\n   172→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-24\",\n   173→    \"topics\": [\n   174→      \"AI agents\",\n   175→      \"scientific automation\",\n   176→      \"research workflows\",\n   177→      \"agentic AI\",\n   178→      \"arXiv\"\n   179→    ],\n   180→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   181→  },\n   182→  {\n   183→    \"title\": \"Separable Expert Architecture: Privacy-Preserving LLM Personalization\",\n   184→    \"description\": \"Paper proposing a separable expert architecture for privacy-preserving LLM personalization via composable adapters and deletable user proxies, addressing privacy concerns in personalized AI systems.\",\n   185→    \"author\": \"Chris Schneider, Philipp Schoenegger, Ben Bariach\",\n   186→    \"source\": \"Paper\",\n   187→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21571\",\n   188→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-24\",\n   189→    \"topics\": [\n   190→      \"LLM personalization\",\n   191→      \"privacy\",\n   192→      \"adapters\",\n   193→      \"model architecture\",\n   194→      \"arXiv\"\n   195→    ],\n   196→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   197→  },\n   198→  {\n   199→    \"title\": \"Thinking with Reasoning Skills: Fewer Tokens, More Accuracy\",\n   200→    \"description\": \"Paper proposing methods to achieve higher accuracy with fewer tokens by focusing on reasoning skills, suggesting efficiency improvements in LLM inference for ACL 2026 Industry Track.\",\n   201→    \"author\": \"Guangxiang Zhao, Qilong Shi, Xusen Xiao, Xiangzheng Zhang, Tong Yang, Lin Sun\",\n   202→    \"source\": \"Paper\",\n   203→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21764\",\n   204→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-24\",\n   205→    \"topics\": [\n   206→      \"LLM efficiency\",\n   207→      \"reasoning\",\n   208→      \"token optimization\",\n   209→      \"inference\",\n   210→      \"arXiv\"\n   211→    ],\n   212→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   213→  },\n   214→  {\n   215→    \"title\": \"GPT-5.5 available in OpenAI Codex\",\n   216→    \"description\": \"GPT-5.5 is out, available in OpenAI Codex and rolling out to paid ChatGPT subscribers. Simon found it fast, effective and highly capable—'I ask it to build things and it builds exactly what I ask for.'\",\n   217→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   218→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   219→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/23/gpt-5.5/\",\n   220→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-23\",\n   221→    \"topics\": [\n   222→      \"OpenAI\",\n   223→      \"GPT-5.5\",\n   224→      \"coding agents\",\n   225→      \"model releases\"\n   226→    ],\n   227→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   228→  },\n   229→  {\n   230→    \"title\": \"LiteParse for the web: PDF text extraction in browser\",\n   231→    \"description\": \"Simon ported LlamaIndex's LiteParse PDF extraction to run entirely in the browser using the same libraries as the Node.js version, enabling client-side PDF text extraction without server.\",\n   232→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   233→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   234→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/23/liteparse-web/\",\n   235→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-23\",\n   236→    \"topics\": [\n   237→      \"PDF parsing\",\n   238→      \"browser-based AI\",\n   239→      \"LlamaIndex\",\n   240→      \"client-side processing\"\n   241→    ],\n   242→    \"type\": \"tutorial\"\n   243→  },\n   244→  {\n   245→    \"title\": \"Qwen3.6-27B: Flagship-Level Coding in a 27B Dense Model\",\n   246→    \"description\": \"Qwen released a 27B dense model claiming flagship-level agentic coding performance, surpassing their previous 397B MoE model. Simon tested the quantized version (16.8GB) locally with excellent results on SVG generation tasks, achieving 25 tokens/sec generation speed.\",\n   247→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   248→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   249→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/qwen3.6-27b/\",\n   250→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-22\",\n   251→    \"topics\": [\n   252→      \"open-source LLM\",\n   253→      \"local LLM\",\n   254→      \"coding agents\",\n   255→      \"Qwen\",\n   256→      \"quantization\"\n   257→    ],\n   258→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   259→  },\n   260→  {\n   261→    \"title\": \"GitHub Copilot pricing changes reflect agentic compute demands\",\n   262→    \"description\": \"GitHub changed Copilot Individual plans: tightening usage limits, pausing signups, restricting Opus 4.7 to Pro+ plan. Simon notes that agentic workflows fundamentally changed compute demands—long-running parallelized sessions consume far more resources than original plan structures supported.\",\n   263→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   264→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   265→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/github-copilot-pricing/\",\n   266→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-22\",\n   267→    \"topics\": [\n   268→      \"GitHub Copilot\",\n   269→      \"pricing\",\n   270→      \"coding agents\",\n   271→      \"compute costs\",\n   272→      \"agentic workflows\"\n   273→    ],\n   274→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   275→  },\n   276→  {\n   277→    \"title\": \"Firefox 150: Claude Mythos Preview found 271 vulnerabilities\",\n   278→    \"description\": \"Mozilla applied Claude Mythos Preview to Firefox and found/fixed 271 vulnerabilities in Firefox 150. CTO Bobby Holley: 'Defenders finally have a chance to win, decisively.' Shows AI's potential for security work.\",\n   279→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (citing Bobby Holley)\",\n   280→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   281→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/firefox-mythos/\",\n   282→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-22\",\n   283→    \"topics\": [\n   284→      \"AI security\",\n   285→      \"Claude\",\n   286→      \"Mozilla Firefox\",\n   287→      \"vulnerability detection\"\n   288→    ],\n   289→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   290→  },\n   291→  {\n   292→    \"title\": \"Claude Code $100/month pricing confusion\",\n   293→    \"description\": \"Anthropic quietly updated pricing page to show $100/month plan, then reverted. Simon analyzes the confusion around Claude Code pricing and compares to GitHub Copilot's recent pricing changes.\",\n   294→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   295→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   296→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/22/claude-code-100/\",\n   297→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-22\",\n   298→    \"topics\": [\n   299→      \"Claude\",\n   300→      \"pricing\",\n   301→      \"coding agents\",\n   302→      \"Anthropic\"\n   303→    ],\n   304→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   305→  },\n   306→  {\n   307→    \"title\": \"ChatGPT Images 2.0: GPT-3 to GPT-5 level leap\",\n   308→    \"description\": \"OpenAI released ChatGPT Images 2.0. Sam Altman claimed the leap from gpt-image-1 to gpt-image-2 is equivalent to jumping from GPT-3 to GPT-5. Simon tested it with image generation prompts.\",\n   309→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   310→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   311→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/21/chatgpt-images-2/\",\n   312→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-21\",\n   313→    \"topics\": [\n   314→      \"OpenAI\",\n   315→      \"image generation\",\n   316→      \"GPT-5\",\n   317→      \"AI art\"\n   318→    ],\n   319→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   320→  },\n   321→  {\n   322→    \"title\": \"Less human AI agents, please\",\n   323→    \"description\": \"Andreas Påhlsson-Notini argues current AI agents are too human in the wrong ways—lacking stringency, patience, focus. They drift toward familiar tasks and negotiate with constraints instead of executing reliably.\",\n   324→    \"author\": \"Andreas Påhlsson-Notini (via Simon Willison)\",\n   325→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   326→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/21/less-human-ai/\",\n   327→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-21\",\n   328→    \"topics\": [\n   329→      \"AI agents\",\n   330→      \"prompt engineering\",\n   331→      \"agent behavior\",\n   332→      \"AI reliability\"\n   333→    ],\n   334→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   335→  },\n   336→  {\n   337→    \"title\": \"Claude Opus 4.7 tokenizer increases token counts by 1.46x\",\n   338→    \"description\": \"Simon built a token counter comparison tool and found Opus 4.7's new tokenizer uses 1.46x more tokens than 4.6 for the same text. For high-res images, the increase is 3x (but only due to supporting higher resolutions). This means effective 40% price increase despite same per-token pricing.\",\n   339→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   340→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   341→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/20/claude-token-counter/\",\n   342→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-20\",\n   343→    \"topics\": [\n   344→      \"Claude\",\n   345→      \"tokenization\",\n   346→      \"pricing\",\n   347→      \"LLM costs\",\n   348→      \"vision\"\n   349→    ],\n   350→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   351→  },\n   352→  {\n   353→    \"title\": \"Headless everything for personal AI\",\n   354→    \"description\": \"Matt Webb and Marc Benioff predict headless services will become common as personal AIs interact via APIs rather than GUIs. Salesforce Headless 360 exposes everything as API/MCP/CLI. This could disrupt per-head SaaS pricing.\",\n   355→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (citing Matt Webb, Marc Benioff)\",\n   356→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   357→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Apr/19/headless-everything/\",\n   358→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-19\",\n   359→    \"topics\": [\n   360→      \"APIs\",\n   361→      \"AI agents\",\n   362→      \"SaaS pricing\",\n   363→      \"headless architecture\"\n   364→    ],\n   365→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   366→  }\n   367→]\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
